\section{Why they exist}{

Output checkers send output back for the same few reasons again and
again. OpenSAFELY reports that about 30 \% of rejected requests have
counts that are not rounded and 20 \% have small counts that are not, or
not correctly, suppressed (\url{https://docs.opensafely.org/output-checking/}).
Suppression is easy to get wrong: a suppressed cell next to a total is
the total minus the other cells. \code{\link[=inspect]{inspect()}} finds these mistakes, but
only after the script has run on the real data. These functions do it
right in the script, and \code{\link[=trial_run]{trial_run()}} shows the result on the copy.
}

\section{Which rules apply}{

Without \code{config} the functions use the configuration that \code{\link[=trial_run]{trial_run()}}
and \code{\link[=real_run]{real_run()}} were given: they place it in the script's session as the
option \code{mimic.config}. The script then follows the rules staff use,
without knowing them. Outside those functions the defaults of
\code{\link[=mimic_config]{mimic_config()}} apply.

Where researchers work on a researcher machine in the secure
environment and run their scripts themselves, staff can set the option
for every session in the machine's \code{Rprofile.site}:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{options(mimic.config = mimic::mimic_config(min_count = 5))
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

With \code{\link[=describe_output]{describe_output()}} the researcher also says what each file shows,
so the checker does not have to guess.
